# END(3)

## NAME

end, etext, edata - last locations in program

## SYNOPSIS

```c
extern char end[];
extern char etext[];
extern char edata[];
```

## DESCRIPTION

These names refer neither to routines nor to locations with interesting contents. The address
of *etext* is the first address above the program text, *edata* above the initialized data
region, and *end* above the uninitialized data region.

When execution begins, the program break coincides with *end,* but many functions reset the
program break, among them the routines of brk(2), malloc(3) and standard input/output
(stdio(3)). The current value of the program break is reliably returned by `sbrk(0)`, see
brk(2). **Note:** v7 also listed the profiling option of cc(1); there is no profiling runtime
here.

## SEE ALSO

brk(2), malloc(3)

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The segment order is const, text, data, bss

Four segments, not the PDP-11's three: a *const* segment holds string literals and initialized
file-scope arrays, ahead of the text. That is why the size check on a program sums
**const+text+data+bss** and why a **.dis** listing begins below the entry point.

### Declare it as an array

**extern char end[];** and not **extern char end;**. The decay of an array name produces a
genuine fat **char \*** at byte offset 0, where taking the address of a **char** object would
produce one describing that one byte. Sbrk(3) declares it exactly that way for exactly that
reason.

### bss is the segment that can outrun a short address

An address field in an instruction reaches [0..07777] and [070000..077777]; the segment order
above puts bss last, so it is the one segment a large program can push past the 12-bit field.
Assembly that names a bss symbol uses the **< >** address escape; *crt0* and *cerror* both say
so at the point where they do it.

A separate ceiling applies to the whole program: text plus data plus bss must fit below the
stack at 070000 - 28,672 words - and no relocatable symbol may sit above word 32,767, the reach
of a 15-bit pointer. Both failures are silent, so the build checks them with **b6size -w**.

## FILES

share/besm6/lib/crt0.o, lib/libc/csu/crt0.s
